chment and we saw that early  suppression of host immunity does drive  this tolerant phenotype  or lethal phenotype again you can see it  day three post infection  and this is a combination of the the  genes that are expressed as well as  micro rnas  which regulate those genes being  expressed that there is a profound  suppression  of um immune and inflammatory responses  in lethal animals at day three  compared to tolerant animals in which  many of these are  upregulated key thing to note is that  many of these things at later time  points are actually down regulated  intolerant animals which suggests that  while lethal animals have no  early response the early response  intolerant animals  allows them to better regulate these  responses  it's thought that severe ebola virus  disease is caused by  unregulated expression of these innate  immune  and inflammatory factors that cause a  systemic inflammatory condition to occur  in the tolerant animals those early  responses allow that response to be  controlled  whereas in the lethal case those  controls are completely removed  and so there's later a complete  unregulated up regulation of  inflammation  which causes disease now i'm going to  switch gears a little bit and talk about  how we can use these host responses to  predict disease outcome so we already  know which  which of these host responses are  associated with either tolerance or  lethality  is there any way that we could use this  information to predict  outcome including in patients and the  way we decided to approach this was  using a machine  learning approach called random forests  random forests are a binary decision  tree method  of classification that allows you to  take  gene profiles and and build a model  that can discriminate on either a lethal  or a tolerant outcome  using those gene expression profiles  when we did this random forest method  with a  profile of about 14 genes from spleen or  liver  and collaborative cross mice we got  extremely