 of  pathogenesis once these models have been  established  we can do transmission or infection root  studies  to understand how susceptible an animal  or a person would be by a given  infection root  we can look at persistence which is  something that is an  open question for stars coronavirus 2  it's something that has only been  appreciated for many other acute viral  infections such as ebola  in recent years when there's been  evidence of persistence in human  patients  and finally we can use these models to  test novel drugs vaccines and  diagnostics  something that again is of critical  importance given the ongoing sars  coronavirus 2  covet 19 pandemic  so as promised i'm going to end by  speaking briefly  about some efforts that i have planned  with collaborators to model sars  coronavirus 2 in the collaborative cross  model  so one thing that's important to note is  that sars coronavirus 2 the wild type  virus cannot use mouse ace2 as a  receptor  ace2 is the molecule that the virus has  to bind in order to enter the cell and  cause an infection  there are transgenic mice that express  the human ac2 receptor  and they can be infected but they do not  develop severe disease or at least they  develop severe disease in a background  dependent model  that suggests that a genetically diverse  model such as the collaborative cross  might actually be much more appropriate  now ace2 human ace2 is located on the x  chromosome this is important because  human ace2 transgenic mice are what are  called nokian mice that means that ace2  the human ace2 transgene has been  inserted  and replaces the mouse gene for ace two  that's on the x chromosome  this also means that if you crossed a  collaborative cross mouse  with an a human ace2 transgenic nokian  mouse  all of the offspring would be able to  express eight ace two  because they all have an x chromosome  so we are planning to make what are  called f1  human ace2 transgenic collaborative  cross mice  this is where we take a female ace2  transgenic mouse which will have a copy  of the human ace2 on each x chromosome  and we cross these with male  collaborative cross mice  those offspring will both have the  genetic diversity  present in the collaborative cross and  will also express  human ace too thus rendering them  susceptible to sars coronavirus ii  infection  our plan is to use this model to look at  the diverse array of host responses  in different genetic backgrounds and  determine how this relates to disease  pathogenesis  and covid19 severity  so finally i'd like to acknowledge my  many collaborators without whom this  work  would not have been possible as well as  my funding sponsors  and i'm happy to answer any questions  that are sent to me after this talk  thank you so much for your time and  attention
 

